// [expr.sizeof]/5 — `sizeof...(P)` yields the pack's ARITY.
//
// evaluate_type_query parses the sizeof OPERAND and implements no `...` arm, so
// the operator is honoured by FOLDING it during substitution. (`sizeof` IS a
// keyword — a soft-reserved TokenCppKeyword that is_contextual_identifier_token
// deliberately admits — so an unfolded one degrades to a VARIABLE lookup rather
// than a syntax error, which is what made this failure silent.)
// The free-function-template path has two
// branches — single-element, and pack_elems.size() >= 2 — and only the single
// one carried the fold. At arity 1 this test would pass with the bug present,
// which is exactly why no existing variadic gate caught it: the multi branch
// sets multi_done and skips the loop the fold lived in.
//
// So this gate expands TWO and THREE elements. With the fold missing from the
// multi branch, `sizeof` survives substitution as a bare identifier, the
// instantiation throws "Expecting '(' or identifier after sizeof", the
// placeholder is kept SILENTLY, and the program dies at MIR link with
// "import of undefined item probe" — an error naming neither sizeof nor the
// template body.
//
// one=1 is the lower bound (the branch that always worked); two=2 and three=3
// are the ones that regressed. A void-returning case is included because the
// first reducer that found this printed from a void function, and the return
// type turned out to be irrelevant — keeping it pins that.
//
// Oracle: g++-13 and clang++-18 both print "one=1 two=2 three=3 void=2".
#include <cstdio>

template <class... Tf>
int count(Tf... t)
{
	return (int)sizeof...(Tf);
}

template <class... Tf>
void vcount(Tf... t)
{
	printf("void=%d\n", (int)sizeof...(Tf));
}

int main()
{
	printf("one=%d\n", count(1));
	printf("two=%d\n", count(1, 2L));
	printf("three=%d\n", count(1, 2L, 'c'));
	vcount(1, 2L);
	return 0;
}
